    "description": "The Order API accepts order-placement commands from the storefront and writes them to the Order Command Queue. Ten Pricing Worker instances run behind that queue, and exactly one instance must calculate tax for each command. If every instance received a copy, the order ledger could contain duplicate charges; if the API selected a named worker, the request path would depend on that worker's identity and availability. The broker may acknowledge a command only after a worker records the result, and unacknowledged work must be available to another instance after a worker crash. Commands older than the allowed processing window must be diverted for diagnosis rather than repeatedly blocking newer orders. The team needs to add worker capacity during promotions without changing the Order API or introducing coordination between workers.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how each order command reaches exactly one processing worker while multiple interchangeable workers can scale horizontally and recover unfinished work after failures.",
    "notes": "Primary point-to-point-channel because each command must reach exactly one worker with the channel owning the assignment; competing-consumers stays as the near miss for the ten-instance worker pool and guaranteed-delivery as supporting for the acknowledge-after-record recovery behavior."
  },
